development.question_text:
Between which two consecutive whole numbers does $\sqrt{7}$ lie?
Use your answer to explain why $\sqrt{7}$ is a surd.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-0.5,0)--(6.5,0);
\foreach \x in {0,1,2,3,4,5,6} {\draw (\x,0.13)--(\x,-0.13); \node[below] at (\x,-0.15) {$\x$};}
\foreach \x in {0.5,1.5,2.5,3.5,4.5,5.5} {\draw (\x,0.07)--(\x,-0.07);}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.solution_text:
$2^{2}=4$ and $3^{2}=9$, and $4<7<9$, so $\sqrt{7}$ lies between $2$ and $3$.
No whole number sits strictly between $2$ and $3$, so no whole number squares to give $7$.
Therefore $7$ is not a perfect square and $\sqrt{7}$ is a surd.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-0.5,0)--(6.5,0);
\foreach \x in {0,1,2,3,4,5,6} {\draw (\x,0.13)--(\x,-0.13); \node[below] at (\x,-0.15) {$\x$};}
\foreach \x in {0.5,1.5,2.5,3.5,4.5,5.5} {\draw (\x,0.07)--(\x,-0.07);}
\fill (2.65,0) circle (2.2pt);
\node[above] at (2.65,0.18) {$\sqrt{7}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

